There are two ways to resolve this, either adopt the Theming API or continue with the legacy themes.

### Adopt the Theming API

Remove the legacy CSS, then pass a theme object to the `theme` grid option. Follow the steps in [Adopt the Theming API](./theming-migration/#adopt-the-theming-api).

Only `ag-grid.css` sets the marker the grid detects, so removing it silences the message. Remove the `ag-theme-*.css` files along with it, or you will be left with part of a legacy theme applied over the new one.

The import is easy to miss, because it can be in any of these places:

- A side-effect import in an entry file or component, such as `import 'ag-grid-community/styles/ag-grid.css'`.
- A `@use` or `@import` in a global stylesheet.
- A `<link>` tag in your HTML, often pointing at a copy of the file rather than the package.
- A build configuration, such as the `styles` array in `angular.json`, or global CSS registered by a framework's app shell.

### Continue with legacy Themes

Pass the string `legacy` to the `theme` grid option and keep the CSS files as they are. See [Continue with Legacy Themes](./theming-migration/#continue-with-legacy-themes).

Where you have several grids, a [Global Grid Option](./grid-interface/#global-grid-options) sets this once for all of them:

```ts
import { provideGlobalGridOptions } from 'ag-grid-community';

// Mark all grids as using legacy themes
provideGlobalGridOptions({ theme: 'legacy' });
```

{% note %}
For the full picture, see [Migrating to the Theming API](./theming-migration).
{% /note %}
